  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nThunderstorm potential returns Friday night\n\nFriday into Saturday, longwave upper trough will advance over Canada \nwith this feature extending southward over the Upper Midwest. Weak \nsurface ridge in the eastern CONUS should lead to southerly moisture \nfetch, setting the stage for a chance (30-60%) of showers and \nthunderstorms as the associated surface front pushes southeast. With \nwesterly winds aloft ramping up on the equator-ward side of the \nupper trough, plenty of shear looks to be available for organized \nconvection. However, most guidance bring the front through the CWA \nby mid-morning Saturday, leading to little in the way of \ninstability. Therefore, current thinking is that the potential for a \nsevere thunderstorm is low (<10%) and, if it were to occur, would \nfavor far southwest Wisconsin early Saturday afternoon before the \nfront fully clears the CWA.\n\nRainy Monday with flooding potential\n\nMoist advection resumes rather quickly following Saturday's frontal \npassage with this ramping up considerably into Monday. 09.00z \nNAEFS/ECENS PWAT values increase to the 90th and 99.5th percentile \nrespectively before cooling aloft ahead of another longwave trough \nand increasing WAA lead to widespread rain. Moderate (2\"+) amounts \nof rainfall appear to be good bet north of I-90 (55-70%). While the \noverall system is progressive and thus flood risk would be limited \nby duration of the event, at least the 09.00z EC depicts rain \ndeveloping along the warm front much earlier than other guidance. \nThis seems to be reflected in 09.00z ECENS EFI output which shows an \narea where many members are toward the 80th-90th percentile \nclimatologically for QPF, as expected given plenty of forcing and \nmoisture as discussed above, with a few more extreme members which \nwould represent the potential earlier start to rain. At this time, \nthis output is mainly within west central Wisconsin north of I-90. \nIf this were to occur, could see a risk for flooding develop. Will \nneed to continue watching this period closely over the coming days.",
